---
// /case-studies/readonlyrest-builds — v5 plan §4.4.
// Simone narrates the in-progress migration first-person.
// This is the most important page on the site: it's where the
// engineering credibility lives. The bug log IS the proof.

import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '../../components/JsonLd.astro';
import CompressionResultsTable from '../../components/CompressionResultsTable.astro';
import {
  webPageSchema,
  breadcrumbListSchema,
  articleSchema,
} from '../../lib/seo';

const path = '/case-studies/readonlyrest-builds';
const title =
  "Migrating ReadonlyREST's 1.71 TB build catalog off AWS S3";
const description =
  'Engineering retrospective: 145 versions, 8 years of release history, ~103,088 objects moved to Hetzner and stored roughly 1,270% smaller. Per-version compression results and the bug log.';

// Article publication metadata.
const datePublished = '2026-05-14';
const dateModified = '2026-08-05';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogType="article">
  <Fragment slot="json-ld">
    <JsonLd schema={webPageSchema({ path, title, description, dateModified })} />
    <JsonLd
      schema={breadcrumbListSchema([
        { name: 'DeltaGlider', path: '/' },
        { name: 'Case studies', path: '/case-studies' },
        { name: "ReadonlyREST builds migration", path },
      ])}
    />
    <JsonLd
      schema={articleSchema({
        path,
        headline: title,
        description,
        authorName: 'Simone Scarduzio',
        authorUrl: 'https://beshu.tech',
        datePublished,
        dateModified,
      })}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <h1>Migrating ReadonlyREST's build catalog off AWS S3</h1>

      <p class="byline">By Simone Scarduzio, founder, Beshu Tech</p>

      <p class="hero-lede">
        For eight years, every ReadonlyREST release uploaded another set
        of Kibana plugin bundles to AWS S3. By the time I looked, the
        catalog had grown to 145 versions — about 103,000 objects,
        1.71&nbsp;TB — and we were paying to keep every byte of it,
        month after month. This is the story of moving all of it to
        Hetzner Object Storage through DeltaGlider: why, how, what it
        cost, and the pitfalls worth knowing if you try the same.
      </p>

      <aside class="result" aria-label="Final migration result">
        <div class="result-stat" role="img" aria-label="1.71 terabytes reduced to 134 gigabytes">
          <span class="result-src">1.71&nbsp;TB</span>
          <span class="result-arrow">→</span>
          <span class="result-dst">134&nbsp;GB</span>
        </div>
        <p class="result-note">
          The migration is complete. The whole catalog — plus every
          release shipped since, 174 versions today — is stored roughly
          1,270% smaller, and serves production downloads through
          DeltaGlider today.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="shell">
    <section class="why">
      <h2>Why we finally moved it</h2>

      <p>
        A build catalog is a strange kind of data. Nobody touches it for
        months, and then a customer on an old Elasticsearch version
        needs one exact plugin build — and it has to be there,
        byte-perfect. So it sits in a bucket, grows with every release,
        and the bill grows with it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The wasteful part is what those releases contain. Each version
        ships nearly the same set of plugin bundles as the one before
        it, with a thin layer of changed code inside. Across the 145
        releases from 1.12.x to 1.69.0 we uploaded near-identical files
        again and again — and paid full price to store every copy,
        because S3 neither knows nor cares that the bytes repeat.
      </p>

      <p>
        I picked Hetzner Object Storage as the destination because it is
        the cheapest S3-compatible option that fit: cold-tier-friendly
        pricing, EU jurisdiction, and no enterprise sales process. Beshu
        Tech has no partnership with Hetzner; it's a vendor choice, not
        a sponsored one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="shape">
      <h2>The setup</h2>

      <p>
        The whole rig was one small EC2 instance in the same region as
        the source bucket, so reading from S3 was free:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Source:</strong>
          <code>s3://readonlyrest-data/build/</code> (AWS S3, eu-west-1)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Destination:</strong>
          <code>s3://beshu/ror/builds/</code> (Hetzner, hel1)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Runner:</strong> single <code>t4g.medium</code> EC2
          in eu-west-1 (intra-region read = no AWS egress charge)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pipeline per version:</strong>
          <code>aws s3 sync</code> → merge
          <code>enterprise/free/pro/*</code> into
          <code>legacy/</code> →
          <code>deltaglider cp -r</code> to Hetzner
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cold storage:</strong> the older 99 versions were
          asleep on Glacier Deep Archive
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The run itself was pleasantly boring. The Glacier versions had
        to be woken up first — a Bulk-tier restore, about $2 for all
        ninety-nine. From there, each version followed the same loop:
        sync it down, merge the enterprise, free, and pro variants into
        one layout, and push it to Hetzner through
        <code>deltaglider cp</code>. The entire migration cost roughly
        $9 of EC2 compute.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="results">
      <h2>The results</h2>

      <p>
        Every number below is verified: each migrated version was hashed
        at the source, round-tripped back through DeltaGlider, and
        hashed again. Three versions tell the story, and the last row is
        the whole catalog:
      </p>

      <CompressionResultsTable detailed={true} includeTotalRow={true} />

      <p>
        The outlier is the interesting one. 1.38.0 compressed worse than
        the rest because its <code>legacy/</code> folder mixes Kibana
        plugins for Elasticsearch 6, 7, and 8 — three families of files
        that are not similar to <em>each other</em>, so the delta
        encoder finds less repetition to exploit. The root folder stored
        1,520% smaller; the legacy folder only 270%. The admin UI's
        Delta Efficiency Panel flags exactly this pattern ("Fair, near
        Poor"), so you can spot it in your own buckets before it costs
        you anything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bugs">
      <h2>Pitfalls worth knowing</h2>

      <p>
        Four things tripped me up along the way. None of them was
        serious, and every one has a fix you can copy:
      </p>

      <h3>The CLI wrote its temp files to <code>/tmp</code></h3>

      <p>
        The Python deltaglider 6.1.1 CLI hardcoded <code>/tmp</code>
        for its working files, and <code>/tmp</code> on my EC2 was a
        1.9 GB <code>tmpfs</code> — a mid-size version ran it out of
        space. I patched it to honor <code>TMPDIR</code> and sent the
        fix upstream.
      </p>

      <h3>A 30 GB disk wasn't enough</h3>

      <p>
        Staging 13 GB of source next to xdelta3's temporary files burst
        the 30 GB volume at the first multi-gigabyte version. Growing it
        to 100 GB cost about $8 per month for the migration window —
        cheap insurance.
      </p>

      <h3><code>pipefail</code> and <code>head</code> don't mix</h3>

      <p>
        My script had <code>set -o pipefail</code> and piped
        deltaglider's output through <code>grep -E ... | head -200</code>.
        After the 200th matching line, <code>head</code> closed the
        pipe, <code>pipefail</code> turned that into an exit, and the
        migration stopped silently mid-upload. Dropping the
        <code>| head</code> made the logs noisier and the script honest.
      </p>

      <h3>Reverse proxies default to 60-second timeouts</h3>

      <p>
        The production deployment sits behind Traefik, whose default
        <code>respondingTimeouts.readTimeout</code> is 60 seconds.
        Multi-gigabyte uploads over the internet died at exactly the
        60-second mark with a 502.
      </p>

      <p>
        I raised it to 30 minutes and wrote the gotcha into
        <a href="/docs/how-to/troubleshooting">the troubleshooting
        guide</a>, because every reverse proxy's default is wrong for
        large S3 uploads.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="verification">
      <h2>Checking the bytes came back right</h2>

      <p>
        Compression is worthless if the data comes back wrong, so every
        migrated version went through the same three checks:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          SHA-256 of the source object (computed by
          <code>aws s3api head-object</code>)
        </li>
        <li>
          SHA-256 of the round-tripped object via the DeltaGlider
          proxy (GET, hash, compare)
        </li>
        <li>
          Manifest check: same object count, same total source size
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        No exceptions logged. Bit-perfect round-trip on every
        sampled object, across all three folders (root, legacy, universal).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="reproducibility">
      <h2>Reproducibility</h2>

      <p>
        Available on request
        (<a href="mailto:contact@beshu.tech?subject=DeltaGlider%20-%20ROR%20migration%20artifacts">contact@beshu.tech</a>):
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>The migration script</li>
        <li>The per-version manifest (CSV)</li>
        <li>The SHA-256 verification script and raw results</li>
      </ul>

    </section>

    <section class="cta">
      <h2>Try the same migration on your data</h2>

      <div class="hero-ctas">
        <a href="/pricing#calculator" class="btn btn-primary">Calculate your savings</a>
        <a href="/trial" class="btn">Start a 30-day trial</a>
        <a href="/docs/tutorials/first-delta-savings" class="btn">Run it free</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* One centered reading column: the article measure IS the shell on
     this page, so text, table, and headings share the same edges. */
  .shell {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.3rem, 4.8vw, 3.3rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .byline {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-top: -0.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  /* Final-result callout — the page's headline fact. */
  .result {
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
    padding: 1.6rem 1.8rem;
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--brand-line-soft);
    border-radius: 14px;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-md), 0 0 28px var(--brand-tint);
  }

  .result-stat {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .result-src {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-decoration: line-through;
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }

  .result-arrow {
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 2.5vw, 1.7rem);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  .result-dst {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 2.8rem);
    color: var(--brand);
    text-shadow: 0 0 24px var(--brand-line);
  }

  .result-note {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    color: var(--ink-strong);
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .why,
  .shape,
  .results,
  .bugs,
  .verification,
  .reproducibility,
  .cta {
    margin: 3.5rem 0;
  }

  .bugs h3 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink-strong);
  }

  /* On wide screens, let the data table breathe beyond the text measure —
     symmetric bleed keeps the column centered. */
  @media (min-width: 1080px) {
    .results :global(.table-scroll) {
      --bleed: 7rem;
      width: calc(100% + 2 * var(--bleed));
      margin-left: calc(-1 * var(--bleed));
      margin-right: calc(-1 * var(--bleed));
    }
  }
</style>
